================================================================================
STRAVA HEATMAP & PMTILES PIPELINE
================================================================================

This directory contains the scripts and tools needed to download heatmaps from
Strava, generate georeferenced map files (GeoTIFF, VRT, JNX), and convert them
into web-optimized PMTiles rasters integrated into the MTB Bergamo Leaflet map.


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
1. WHY PMTILES ARE SPLIT INTO FOUR QUADRANTS (GITHUB LIMITS)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The Bergamo heatmap .pmtiles files are generated split into 4 quadrants
(NordOvest, NordEst, SudOvest, SudEst) due to the following technical reasons:

- HARD GITHUB SINGLE FILE LIMIT (100 MB):
  GitHub strictly rejects and blocks pushes containing any single file larger
  than 100 MB (error: "GH001: Large files detected... exceeds file size limit of
  100.00 MB"). In addition, it issues warnings for files over 50 MB.

- HEATMAP FILE SIZE:
  A single compressed PMTiles file for the entire Bergamo area
  (BergamoRide-YYYYMM.pmtiles) would weigh around 190-200 MB, making it
  impossible to commit and push to GitHub without using Git LFS.

- THE FOUR-QUADRANT SPLIT:
  By splitting the bounding box in half along both latitude and longitude,
  even the densest and heaviest quadrant (SudOvest) stays at around 82 MB,
  safely remaining below the 100 MB hard threshold.

- CLIENT OPTIMIZATION AND GITHUB PAGES:
  In the Leaflet frontend (custom.js), the four files are loaded as distinct
  raster layers grouped into a single L.layerGroup.
  The browser fetches tile chunks via HTTP range requests only for quadrants
  currently visible within the map viewport, reducing memory usage and
  bandwidth consumption (also helping keep traffic within GitHub Pages' 100 GB
  monthly soft bandwidth limit).


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. SCRIPT DESCRIPTIONS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

A) PROXY AND AUTHENTICATION (firefox/ directory):
   Strava requires an authenticated session to download high-resolution
   heatmap tiles (zoom level 15).

   - firefox/PROXY.sh:
     Bash script that launches Firefox with cookie logging enabled (MOZ_LOG).
     Upon closing the browser, it automatically extracts session and CloudFront
     cookies into cookies.txt and starts the local proxy.py server.
   - firefox/cookies.txt:
     Contains the session cookies and CloudFront tokens extracted by PROXY.sh,
     required to authorize Strava heatmap tile requests.
   - firefox/proxy.py:
     Local HTTP proxy server (listening on http://localhost:8080) that
     intercepts tile requests and injects valid Strava cookies and headers.
   - firefox/README:
     Summary of sport activity types, URL paths, and Strava color palettes.

B) GEOTIFF DOWNLOAD AND GENERATION:
   The scripts use the ./stitch binary executable to download tiles through the
   local proxy and assemble them into a georeferenced GeoTIFF (zoom 15).

   Bergamo area scripts:
   - BergamoRide.sh:
     All cycling activities combined (ride, hot palette).
   - BergamoMountainBikeRide.sh:
     Mountain Bike activities only (sport_MountainBikeRide, purple palette).
   - BergamoEMountainBikeRide.sh:
     E-Mountain Bike activities (sport_EMountainBikeRide, purple palette).
   - BergamoRun.sh:
     Running and trail activities (run, bluered palette).
   - BergamoStrava.sh:
     Generic/all-activities Strava heatmap.
   - BergamoGoogle.sh:
     Satellite / hybrid imagery from Google Maps.

   Valle Imagna area scripts:
   - ValleImagnaRide.sh, ValleImagnaRun.sh, ValleImagnaStrava.sh,
     ValleImagnaGoogle.sh:
     Same as above, but restricted to the Valle Imagna bounding box for lighter
     maps tailored to Garmin GPS devices (.jnx).

   Each download script automatically produces:
   - ${NAME}.tif:  Tiled GeoTIFF (TILED=YES) with LZW compression.
   - ${NAME}.vrt:  QGIS Virtual Raster with zoom pyramids (.vrt.ovr).
   - ${NAME}.jnx:  Garmin BirdsEye raster map (created with qmt_map2jnx).

C) PMTILES CONVERSION:
   - tif2pmtiles-webp-4.sh (and symlink tif2pmtiles_split.sh):
     Core script for the website.
     Reads the .tif (or .vrt), calculates the bounding box and midpoints, then
     crops and generates the 4 quadrants with WEBP compression (quality 80)
     and pyramid overviews:
       ${BASE_NAME}-NordOvest.pmtiles
       ${BASE_NAME}-NordEst.pmtiles
       ${BASE_NAME}-SudOvest.pmtiles
       ${BASE_NAME}-SudEst.pmtiles
   - tif2pmtiles-webp.sh:
     Converts the entire area into a single WEBP-compressed .pmtiles file.
   - tif2pmtiles.sh:
     Converts the entire area into a single PNG .pmtiles file.

D) BATCH SCRIPTS (all_*.sh):
   - all_downwload.sh:
     Runs all download scripts in sequence (Valle Imagna and Bergamo).
     Requires the local proxy to be running on port 8080.
   - all_pmtiles.sh:
     Reads the current date (DATE=`date +%Y%m`) and runs tif2pmtiles-webp-4.sh
     in sequence for the three primary layers used on the website:
       BergamoEMountainBikeRide-$DATE
       BergamoMountainBikeRide-$DATE
       BergamoRide-$DATE


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. OPERATIONAL GUIDE
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

UPDATING WITH NEW STRAVA HEATMAPS

1. Start PROXY.sh in a terminal:
     cd strava/firefox
     sh PROXY.sh
   The script automatically opens Firefox with cookie logging enabled.

2. In Firefox, log in to Strava and open/view the heatmap:
   It is essential to pan and view the map so that Strava requests tiles and
   issues the required CloudFront cookies.

3. Close Firefox:
   When the browser window is closed, PROXY.sh automatically extracts the
   captured cookies into cookies.txt and starts the local proxy server on
   http://localhost:8080 (keep this terminal open with the proxy running).

4. In another terminal, download the maps:
     cd strava
     sh all_downwload.sh

5. Generate the PMTiles split into 4 quadrants:
     sh all_pmtiles.sh

6. Move the generated PMTiles files to the web directory:
     mv Bergamo*-Nord*.pmtiles Bergamo*-Sud*.pmtiles ../www/pmtiles/


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. WEBSITE INTEGRATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

In custom.js, the create_bergamo_heatmap function loads the four quadrants:
  name + '-NordEst.pmtiles'
  name + '-NordOvest.pmtiles'
  name + '-SudEst.pmtiles'
  name + '-SudOvest.pmtiles'

When updating the map date (e.g. to BergamoRide-202611):
1. Update layer names in the corresponding custom.js functions:
   - create_bergamoride
   - create_bergamomountainbikeride
   - create_bergamoemountainbikeride
2. From the repository root, rebuild the www/all.js bundle:
     make js
   (Note: Never edit www/all.js directly, as specified in AGENTS.md).


--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. REFERENCES, ARCHIVING & STRAVA DATA TRUNCATION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

- Technical analysis of Strava data truncation (GitHub issue):
  https://github.com/julcnx/strava-heatmap-extension/issues/41#issuecomment-6182879086
  Explains how Strava, with the introduction of new tiles (v=20), subtracted
  the 12 lowest intensity levels pixel-by-pixel compared to legacy tiles:
  grayscale = max(0, legacy - 12). This truncation dropped between 15% and
  65% of visible heat data, wiping out faintly tracked forest trails and
  rarely used paths that are vital for trail and MTB mapping.

- Global heatmap archiving initiative by OSM (Reddit discussion):
  https://www.reddit.com/r/openstreetmap/comments/1xmasb2/call_to_action_to_archive_the_strava_global/
  OpenStreetMap community discussion on the critical need to archive and
  preserve historical heatmaps before further access restrictions, API
  closures, or deliberate data degradation by Strava.
